        "text": "The study is designed to capture both qualitative and quantitative data to provide a comprehensive understanding of the toolkit's implementation in classrooms and to assess participants' AI literacy. We recruited 13 middle and high school teachers who taught mixed subjects. Of these, 54% were teaching in schools in North America and 46% in East Asia, with 62% teaching in public schools and 38% in private schools. The participants had an average of 9.15 years (STDV=9.10) of teaching experience in 9 different subjects. 30% of the teachers had experience teaching Information Technology (IT) courses, a prevalent subject in East Asian K-12 education systems that emphasizes computer usage, programming, and AI literacy, representing a diverse crosssection of educators with varying levels of experience in AI technologies and pedagogy. Participants were selected through purpose-sampling to ensure diverse teaching backgrounds and contexts. Before starting the study, participants were informed about the research goals, procedures, and data privacy protocols. Institutional Review Board (IRB) approval was obtained prior to data collection, and each participant provided informed consent. Demographic data was collected prior to the demo and interviews, which were conducted in English and Mandarin. Teachers received $20 for participating in a one-hour interview, and all 13 sessions were analyzed.",
