        "text": "Beginning and early career teachers are often provided a mentor teacher to help facilitate their growth and transition into the profession. These mentor teachers may also be a valuable resource in helping early career teachers further develop their competencies and interpersonal interactions with their assigned paraprofessionals, provided they also possess this experience. More importantly, district and school administrators can play a part in fostering positive teacher-paraprofessional relationships and support teachers in their supervision of paraprofessionals. School leaders have the power to adjust school schedules to allow for planning, collaboration, and supervision time between teachers and paraprofessionals, which in turn can enhance the quality their relationship. School leaders can also determine and change paraprofessionals assignments as needed. When considering the current study's findings regarding teachers' years of experience, it is within school leaders' authority to correctly match teachers and paraprofessionals. Novice, inexperienced teachers, may have a difficult time interacting with a far more experienced and older paraprofessional without supervision supports. Lastly, school leaders can also address issues that may arise in the teacher paraprofessional relationship. \n\nSchool psychologists may also be ideal candidates for developing and supporting teachers' competencies in supervising and collaborating with their paraprofessionals given their integral role in facilitating school personnels' involvement in the provision of student supports. School psychologists can provide school personnel training and consultation/coaching support to enhance role clarity of paraprofessionals, best supervisory practices, and working partnerships. Additionally, school psychologists can assist school leaders and classroom teachers on hiring and developing paraprofessionals skills in meeting classroom and student specific needs across contexts (general education, special education). The observed differences in teacher perceptions based on contextual factors in the current study may warrant special consideration from school psychologists. It may be helpful for school psychologists to offer extra facilitative support with less experienced teachers given their relatively lower appraisal of paraprofessional-teacher relationships in the current study. Along these lines, school psychologists are also well positioned to facilitate conversations regarding diversity and cultural humility. The current study's finding suggests that cultural factors may influence teachers' relationship with their paraprofessional, particularly Latinx/Hispanic teachers who evidenced lower Joining ratings with their paraprofessional.",
