      "snippet": {
        "text": "Paraprofessionals, also known as paraeducators, teacher assistants, instructional aides, and many other titles, are commonly relied upon by school districts to provide learning, behavioral, and social-emotional support services to students with disabilities. Over time, this profession has proliferated from as little as 10,000 persons in the 1960's (Green & Barnes, 1989;Pickett, 1986) to as many as 488,000 paraprofessionals serving students with disabilities (U.S. Department of Education, 2018), and perhaps as many as 1,300,000 persons when considering their many various titles and roles (U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2017). As the number of paraprofessionals have increased over time, their roles in classroom settings have expanded beyond clerical-administrative duties (e.g., photocopying, materials preparation, hall monitoring) towards more significant involvement in facilitating student learning. For example, paraprofessionals now assist in the delivery of assigned programs and services for specific students with disabilities, facilitating one-on-one instruction, delivering behavioral and social support to students, and communicating with parents and child study teams about student progress (Brock & Carter, 2015;Giangreco et al., 2011;Stewart, 2019). This is especially true in the context of special education classrooms, where multiple paraprofessionals may be employed to serve students with significant learning and behavior disabilities, but is also true of paraprofessionals serving in general education classrooms and inclusive classrooms. In the latter types of classrooms, paraprofessionals may be assigned to support a single student or group of students with an IEP, or even the whole classroom to meet adult-to-student capacity needs. Across all settings, paraprofessionals are often tasked with facilitating behavioral management (Irvin et al., 2018) throughout the whole classroom, and may lead small group instruction activities for all students, not just their assigned caseload.",
