      "snippet": {
        "text": "Coaches were another key aspect of the people power these teams reported. The support reported by these coaches include those found in the literature such as facilitating data-driven decision making (Coyne et al., 2024), providing responsive PD (Kraft et al., 2018), and supporting instruction that is contingent on student need (Walpole et al., 2010). It was also apparent that coaches served as an important liaison between building administration, district literacy personnel, school interventionists, and grade-level teams. In this sense, coaches served as a key connection among grade-level team members and other school and district stakeholders. \n\nLastly, school principals were another key aspect of the reported people power. Studies of effective learning environments highlight the key role of school principals. These studies suggest that principals in effective schools develop a collective responsibility for student outcomes (Taylor et al., 2005) and facilitate a productive learning environment within the school (Denton et al., 2003). The teachers in this sample reported robust support from their principals. The reported support includes arranging and subsequently adjusting the school master schedule throughout the year to ensure all students were receiving adequate instruction, purchasing materials requested by the teachers, hiring paraprofessionals to support small-group reading instruction, regularly holding data meetings, and availability to listen to concerns or issues arising in the classroom. \n\nIn elementary schools, and especially in Title I contexts, teachers work with a unique set of challenges and opportunities. With a diverse range of learners and varying levels of support, responsive teaching becomes a cornerstone for success. Other reports have indicated that effective schools utilize collaborations beyond the classroom teachers to promote reading outcomes (Taylor et al., 1999(Taylor et al., , 2000)). In this case, these teachers utilized paraprofessionals, school coaches, and principals to help enact effective reading instruction.",
