      "snippet": {
        "text": "Future research should examine and consider different social and cultural contexts to determine the generalization of findings. For example, future research can include teachers with more diverse backgrounds who work in rural schools, other states, and regions of the US. Likewise, studies that include teachers and paraprofessionals employed in middle school or high school settings are also needed to provide comparisons of their work with students at different grade and developmental levels. Research should focus on measuring how background and cultural factors may influence the supervisor nature and quality of teacher-paraprofessional relationships, with particular attention Latinx teachers' perceptions in the classroom. Future studies that include larger teacher and paraprofessional samples could also match educator characteristics by years of experience, race and/or ethnicity, education degree, and training opportunities. Replication of studies are warranted for teachers and paraprofessionals who support students with other disabilities such as autism or intellectual disabilities as the needs of these students and the subsequent interventions and activities employed by teachers and paraprofessionals are markedly different. Research would benefit from utilizing both quantitative and qualitative measures (i.e., mixed method design studies) which can offer both greater nuance and broader perspective in examining the nature of teacher and paraprofessional relationships. Finally, investigations that use a multi-source and -method assessment approach (e.g., rating scales, interview, systematic direct observation, and social network analyses) to understand relationships qualities and explore possible mediators or moderators of these relationships would be beneficial.",
