      "snippet": {
        "text": "They advocated a hands-on approach where educators would engage in critical thinking to identify and correct potential oversights, such as the misapplication of statistical distributions. In addition to these points, Lee and Yeo (2022) demonstrated the potential of AI-based chatbots to foster higher-order thinking skills by providing personalized, open-ended interactions. Their study showed that chatbots could simulate more authentic teaching contexts, requiring pre-service teachers to critically evaluate situations and formulate independent responses. This approach contrasts with traditional technology-enhanced methods that offer limited decision points and fixed response options, thereby restricting the authenticity of the simulation. The open-ended practice opportunities provided by chatbots allowed pre-service teachers (in the study) to explore student thinking and to evaluate critically teaching scenarios, enhancing their reflective thinking and creativity. Cooper (2023) has further argued that educators should model responsible use of AI, prioritizing critical thinking and ethical considerations in its application. This would include setting clear expectations for its use in assessments and encouraging broader conversations about generative AI's implications. These perspectives suggested that while AI-based chatbots like ChatGPT could be valuable tools for fostering critical thinking and creativity, their effective use might depend on rigorous oversight and an iterative, reflective approach by educators.",
