        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Generative artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a transformative force in education (Mello et al., 2023), revolutionizing classroom dynamics and creating both opportunities and challenges for teachers. A recent American survey revealed that 51% of teachers use ChatGPT, with 40% incorporating it into their routines and 10% utilizing it daily (Government Technology, 2023). Various other Generative AI tools-such as Canva, Gamma, Gemini, Heygen, Padlet, Leonardo, and Ping-are also being used in classrooms (Kyrpa et al., 2024) for various purposes, such as administrative tasks, content creation, and personalized learning. Each of these tools has more specialized benefits, such as enhancing creativity (e.g., Gamma, Canva), improving student engagement (e.g., Padlet, Leonardo, Heygen, ChatGPT, Curipod), and streamlining classroom management (e.g., MagicSchool, Eduaide). While use of Generative AI improves teaching methods and fosters interactive learning environments (Limna et al., 2022) these tools also come with noticeable limitations, including the generation of inaccurate information and limited free access (Kyrpa et al., 2024). \n\nSuccessful implementation of Generative AI in education depends on teachers' awareness and willingness to adopt these tools. Not all teachers use Generative AI in their teaching methods (Kyrpa et al., 2024), and AI integration in educational platforms is progressing slowly in many schools or is entirely absent in many others worldwide (Cukurova et al., 2023). This highlights the importance of understanding factors influencing teacher acceptance and use of Generative AI. While existing research has explored various aspects of AI in education, including applications (Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019;K. Zhang & Aslan, 2021) and implementation challenges (Ji et al., 2023), a significant gap remains in understanding the drivers of teachers' perceptions and adoption of AI tools in school teaching. Furthermore, while some research on AI adoption in higher education exists (Chatterjee & Bhattacharjee, 2020), studies examining AI integration in K-12 settings are scarce.",
