        "text": "Artificial intelligence is the ability of computer systems to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as recognizing patterns, learning from experience, drawing conclusions, and making predictions or recommendations. AI relies on technologies like machine learning, deep learning, and neural networks to enable computers to learn and improve themselves through experience. Generative AI is a type of artificial intelligence focused on generating new content like text, images, video, and audio that mimics human-created material. Generative AI models like GPT-3, DALL-E 2, and Stable Diffusion are trained on vast datasets then generate new outputs based on patterns in the data, allowing for highly realistic synthetic content. The entirety of this paragraph, up to this sentence, was written by ChatGPT. One of the authors accessed it and used prompts, or questions, to obtain a short summary of AI in general, and specifically 'generative AI' . We have integrated generative AI into the introduction of this paper to help demonstrate our approach to using it in an Entrepreneurship class. We view AI as a tool that can augment human Entrepreneurial activity when integrated into new creation activities that were previously exclusively human in origin. \n\nIt has been proposed that AI will change the way Entrepreneurial organizations will operate [9], and disrupt how scholars conduct Entrepreneurship research [6]. One must assume that AI will also disrupt the way Entrepreneurship instructors teach classes. In a Big Bang like event, ChatGPT exploded onto the AI scene in November 2022 [12]. With all the publicity around AI, more specifically Large Language Models (LLMs) like ChatGPT and more broadly generative AI, many Entrepreneurship instructors across the world are wondering what to do. Should we ban ChatGPT in our classes? Should we fully embrace it and let students have a free for all? Should it be a combination of the two? What about plagiarism issues? Will AI replace instructors? While all of these questions cannot be addressed in this paper, in particular the existential ones, we will put forward an argument, based on our experiences, for integrating AI into Entrepreneurship teaching. This will include exercises that can easily be implemented by instructors with no AI experience in virtually any Entrepreneurship course. You do not even need your own ChatGPT account, just the students need to sign up.",
