      "snippet": {
        "text": "A basic and simple definition of Artificial Intelligence(AI) is given by the creator of the AI concept, John McCarthy, in 1955 as \"the science and engineering of making intelligent machines\" [38]. Machine learning (ML) is the primary method used today to make machines intelligent. That's why, we often use the terms AI and ML interchangeably. The term \"Generative AI\" refers to the tools that implement ML techniques to generate new content. When the generated content is textual, we use the term \"Large Language Models\" (LLMs) to specifically refer to Generative AI for texts such as ChatGPT. \n\nThe recent media hype surrounding AI may give the impression that AI is the panacea of all kinds of problems in life, which is not true. It is better for educators to consider that the ideal use of AI is to complement human intelligence in order to understand the intelligence of the universe which humans don't (will never) completely understand. \n\nThe strengths and limitations of AI can be boiled down to the study of the strengths and limitations of machine learning (ML), which includes all the Generative AI tools that educators are concerned about. Therefore, educators should constantly monitor and understand the strengths [39] and limitations of ML. The strengths of ML allow educators to provide personalized education to their students. In this case, the role of educators is to ensure that students are correctly exploiting the strengths of AI for their self-growth, as opposed to overrelying on AI. On the other hand, educators should also constantly be aware of the limitations of ML during the design of their courses. \n\nWe first discuss the limitations of AI and how these limitations can be exploited in course design. Then, we provide different examples to illustrate these limitations. To depict the strengths of AI for education, we present AI-based tools that have been integrated into education before the advent of ChatGPT.",
