      "snippet": {
        "text": "Since the public launch of ChatGPT, researchers have posted a spate of preliminary findings regarding the use of generative AI in various work contexts. The use-cases considered are varied, including conducting background research, idea generation, writing and editing tasks, coding, generating presentations/reports, and acting as a sounding board to provide feedback (Boehnke et al., 2024;Brynjolfsson et al., 2023;Chakrabarty et al., 2023;Chen & Chan, 2023;Choi et al., 2023;Dell'Acqua et al., 2023;Doshi & Hauser, 2023;Girotra et al., 2023;Noy & Zhang, 2023;Peng et al., 2023). Contexts studied included advertising, law, consulting, software programming, creative writing, product design and customer support. While this is early-stage, non-peer-reviewed work, the findings point to a number of consistent patterns across these diverse contexts, types of work and use-cases which help to inform our thoughts on how generative AI may impact creative work. \n\nAlong metrics such as accuracy, efficiency, productivity and average quality, the incorporation of generative AI appears to consistently improve workers' output -particularly for individuals who are novices or lack expertise in a given task. For example, in a randomized controlled trial involving law students, Choi et al. (2023) found that use of GPT substantially reduced the time spent on legal tasks such as contract and complaint drafting and drafting a section of an employee handbook. Studying management consultants through a randomized field experiment, Dell 'Acqua et al. (2023) found that the use of AI significantly increased the speed of task completion and average performance of consultants' work products. Such studies also consistently find that experts do not benefit as much from the use of generative AI in their work performance as novices. For example, in an online experiment involving professional writing tasks, Noy and Zhang (2023) found that, while the use of ChatGPT consistently increased speed of task completion among writers of differing ability, it only improved quality of writing output among the 'low-ability' writers -those who scored lower on an initial writing task.",
