      "snippet": {
        "text": "In the Fourth Industrial Revolution era, digital transformation has become a strategic necessity across various sectors, including public administration (Araujo et al., 2021;Luna-Reyes et al., 2020;Pariyasiri, 2022;Trieu et al., 2023). Governments worldwide are striving to adopt advanced technologies to provide faster, more efficient, and transparent public services. One of the most prominent technological innovations is Artificial Intelligence (AI). AI can process largescale data, provide predictive analysis, and automate complex administrative processes (De Carlo et al., 2020;Kumar et al., 2024;Lee & Yoon, 2021;Tao et al., 2019). According to Chen & Biswas (2021), AI enhances operational efficiency and optimizes user experiences through more personalized and responsive approaches. For instance, using chatbots for direct interaction with the public has enabled governments to offer 24/7 services without requiring the physical presence of staff. Globally, studies by Ji et al., (2023) demonstrate how developed countries like South Korea and Estonia have successfully implemented AI in public service systems, such as managing transportation, document administration, and predictive analytics for citizen needs, resulting in better services at lower costs. \n\nHowever, implementing AI in public administration has significant challenges, especially in developing countries like Indonesia. Seifert & Charness (2022) emphasize that the success of digital transformation depends on infrastructure readiness, organizational adoption of technology, and societal acceptance of change. Meanwhile, Li & Jiang (2022) highlight that developing nations often face major obstacles, including budget constraints, technological disparities between regions, and low digital literacy among government officials. In terms of ethics, Lee & Yoon, (2021) caution that while AI has great potential to improve efficiency, there are risks associated with algorithmic bias, privacy breaches, and lack of transparency in AI-based decision-making processes. These challenges show that AI implementation is not just a technical issue but also requires attention to social, ethical, and policy aspects. \n\nCurrent conditions in Indonesia highlight gaps in the implementation of digital transformation in the public sector.",
