      "snippet": {
        "text": "Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) has become a transformative force in education, offering various opportunities to enhance learning experiences and improve teaching methods while also bringing in many challenges. GenAI, such as ChatGPT, a sophisticated AI chatbot powered by large language models (LLMs), can simulate human-like dialogue, offering personalized tutoring, answering questions, and providing detailed explanations across a wide range of subjects (OpenAI, 2022). ChatGPT can make learning more accessible and engaging, particularly for students who benefit from individualized support (Opara et al., 2023). Additionally, ChatGPT can assist educators by automating routine tasks such as grading and providing feedback, freeing up time for developing more meaningful activities with students (Lo, 2023). However, using GenAI in education also presents challenges, including concerns about data privacy, the potential for bias in GenAI responses, and the need to maintain the critical role of fostering critical thinking and problem-solving skills among future citizens (Zhai, 2022). \n\nAccording to the 2024 position statement on AI from the National Council of Teachers of Mathematics (NCTM), teachers are responsible for building a bridge between students and AI so that students do not simply use it as a substitute for human intelligence. To effectively build this bridge, teachers must first understand students' perceptions and approaches to AI, as these significantly shape the guidelines and strategies teachers develop for implementing AI tools. Research on students' learning processes related to GenAI is needed to provide insights that will enable teachers to more effectively support and guide students in utilizing AI for educational purposes (Kasneci et al., 2023;Yoon et al., 2024). With the increasing use of AI-assisted and AI-enabled technologies in education and daily life, this study explores how contrasting perceptions of ChatGPT in mathematical learning may influence users' interactions with the tool. The findings provide recommendations for integrating AI into mathematics education and inform future research directions.",
