        "text": "Preliminary research on the application of GPT-3 in tutoring programming suggests that AI-driven resources can enhance students' problem-solving abilities, increase engagement, and deepen their understanding of computer programming concepts [6,7]. However, despite these promising results, concerns have been raised regarding the potential over-reliance on AI-generated solutions, which could lead to a diminished focus on fundamental programming logic and critical thinking skills [8]. \n\nDespite the growing body of research supporting the role of AI in education, there remains a lack of comprehensive and structured reviews synthesising evidence from controlled experimental studies [9]. AI research has often been criticised for limitations such as small sample sizes, inconsistent experimental controls, and variations in AI tool functionalities, all of which challenge the generalisability of findings [10]. In addition, AI-assisted programming education encompasses a wide range of tools, from simple code auto-completion assistants to advanced machine learning-driven tutoring systems, making it difficult to draw broad conclusions from isolated studies. These gaps in the literature underscore the need for a systematic review and meta-analysis to aggregate evidence from diverse controlled experimental studies and provide a clearer understanding of how AI tools, particularly ChatGPT, impact learning outcomes in introductory programming courses [11]. \n\nThis systematic review and meta-analysis aims to assess the impact of AI tools, such as ChatGPT, on student learning outcomes in introductory computer programming courses. The primary objectives of this study are to evaluate the effectiveness of AI in improving student performance, engagement, and problem-solving skills, as well as to compare AIassisted learning with traditional instructional methods. Furthermore, this review examines how different AI tool designs, usage patterns, and student interactions influence learning outcomes. By synthesising findings from controlled experimental studies, this review seeks to provide evidence-based recommendations for effectively integrating AI tools into computer programming curricula to maximise their educational benefits while addressing potential challenges.",
