      "snippet": {
        "text": "The findings of this systematic review and meta-analysis strongly indicate that AI tools, including ChatGPT and other computer programming assistants, have a significant positive impact on students' learning outcomes in introductory programming courses. Across 35 studies, which included randomised controlled trials and quasi-experimental designs, strong evidence supports AI tools' contributions to computer programming proficiency, task completion rates, and student engagement. \n\nThese results have profound implications for educational practice, particularly in introductory programming courses. AI tools that provide real-time feedback and problemsolving assistance create a more supportive learning environment. They also help students develop a deeper understanding of computer programming concepts, making AI-powered education a promising approach for improving student outcomes. The ability of AI tools to cater to individual learning needs is particularly valuable in large classrooms, where personalised attention is often limited [25][26][27]. Additionally, increased student engagement and motivation could lead to higher retention rates and long-term success in computer programming education. \n\nOur results align with previous research demonstrating the positive effects of AIbased learning systems on academic achievement in computer programming education. Studies by [14,28] support the conclusion that AI-powered tools significantly enhance learning outcomes. Similar findings have also been reported in other STEM disciplines, where AI-driven learning interventions have led to improvements in student performance and comprehension. \n\nHowever, heterogeneity among the included studies is evident, with some studies reporting only minor effects, while others demonstrate substantial improvements [29][30][31]. This variation in findings may be attributed to differences in AI tool functionalities, implementation quality, and student demographics [32][33][34][35]. For example, Ref. [14] found only a minor improvement, whereas Ref. [28] observed significant learning gains. These discrepancies highlight the complexity of AI integration in education and suggest that its effectiveness is likely context-dependent. The high variation found in the meta-analysis (I 2 = 95% for task completion time, I 2 = 54% for performance scores, and I 2 = 55% for success and ease of understanding) is likely caused by differences in study design, AI tool use, student characteristics, course structures, instructional methods, result measurement, and potential publication bias.",
