      "snippet": {
        "text": "Their use spans a number of day-to-day developer tasks such as code completion, automated documentation, refactoring suggestions, and even test generation [38,43]. To evaluate the impact of these tools on developer productivity, many user studies have been conducted with popular assistants like GitHub Copilot or bespoke tools powered by different LLMs. However, the findings are mixed. \n\nFor example, Weisz et al. [41] found that different users benefited to varying degrees when using AI coding assistants. Ziegler et al. [43] found that developers who used GitHub Copilot reported higher productivity levels. Comparative evaluations across multiple assistants (e.g., Copilot, TabNine, ChatGPT) further demonstrate heterogeneous outcomes in correctness, speed, and acceptance [10]. At the same time, large-scale field studies of AI-enabled code search illustrate that adoption is uneven and effectiveness must outweigh switching costs to drive real productivity benefits [23]. Complementary perspectives even propose physiological or cognitive-load measures, such as those gathered via wearables, as additional dimensions to capture productivity impacts of generative AI [3]. Across these studies, inconsistent metrics are employed to study productivity, leading to different downstream conclusions. Thus, we create a similar framework-like SPACE/DORA-but specifically tailored for AI coding assistants.",
