      "snippet": {
        "text": "This special issue of Communication Teacher highlights the importance of using generative artificial intelligence (AI) tools in communication education. Although the technology is still emerging, we are already witness to the potential impact generative AI can have on teaching and learning activities in the communication discipline and the fields in which communication is an important concern. Throughout the educational ecosystem, individuals, institutions, and industries discover and exploit the affordances of generative AI, often in novel or unforeseen ways. Its influence is inescapable as the number of digital tools with some generative AI capability continues to grow and those around us -especially our students-gain more experience using them. \n\nThrough our practices, we shape generative AI as our ally, our enemy, or both. This issue reflects an important historical moment in this effort as educators experiment and explore how to effectively respond to the sudden presence and availability of this power technology. The articles in this issue showcase the ways in which educators and learners have innovated, adapted, or invented their practices and activities. Although reflecting the state of generative AI available today, the activities presented here have continued relevance even as particular tools change. A strength of these articles is that they are focused on teaching and learning rather than on any specific tool. Each article exemplifies a different approach to using AI through the lens of an enduring education concern, such as course design or student assessment. \n\nThe activities presented in this issue are applicable to a broad range of courses and assignments. They also cover an interesting variety of generative AI tools, from general-purpose tools such as the foundational large language models (LLMs) of ChatGPT or Gemini to image-generating tools (e.g. DALL-E), speech coaches, and chatbot generators. An important and unique contribution of this issue is that it demonstrates a range of attitudes that communication educators have toward generative AI, from those that are most hopeful to those that urge caution and raise important concerns. Altogether, I suggest that they demonstrate four general stances that represent how we might respond to or utilize generative AI in our courses. \n\nThe first stance is one of Observation and critical awareness.",
