        "text": "There are, however, a variety of ways this process can be modified to reduce the time burden for educators. First, an educator could choose to provide feedback only on specific sections of the report and rely on peer feedback for the remainder of the report. Indeed, Davidson's suggestions (2024) reiterate the usefulness of peer feedback on saving instructors' time, but also emphasizes how peer feedback can be less intimidating than instructor feedback (p. 196). \n\nSecond, instructors can consider the possibility of providing feedback and not assigning a grade. Some educators have used the practice of providing feedback throughout the project and only assigning a grade at the end (Mackisack 1994;Chance 1997), reducing the number of tasks an instructor must perform. However, this system may lead to students feeling confused as to what feedback is absolutely necessary for their report to meet certain criteria and what feedback is more nuanced. Thus, we recommend educators considering assigning a mark of \"Present, \" \"Partially Present, \" and \"Missing\" to select components of the grading rubric, indicating if students' work is adhering to the expected criteria. \n\nAlthough this process adds to the workload of educators, allowing students the opportunity to revise even small portions of their report, \"promotes a safe space for students to fail and learn from that experience instead of being defined by it\" (Curley et al. 2024, p. 287).",
