      "snippet": {
        "text": "(3) # Add AI constructive feedback: number of AI-generated constructive comments inserted (shown only when a rubric is not met). (4) # Add AI positive feedback: number of AI-generated praise comments inserted (shown only when a rubric is met). \n\n(5) # Additional feedback: number of extra comment boxes added beyond rubric-anchored comments. Timestamps on each action also allow us to estimate per-essay grading time as the elapsed time between the first and last action on that essay. 5.6.2 Student Essay Quality Analysis. We used an LLM-based rubric scorer to evaluate both first and final drafts. For each rubric item, the model produced a satisfied/missing judgment (0 or 1) via the same rubric-based pipeline as FeedbackWriter; rubric-level scores were then aggregated into a total score using the TAs' weighting scheme. We decided to use the AI-derived scores instead of the TA grades for the following four reasons: \n\n(1) TAs' first-draft grades were provisional. TAs emphasized formative comments on first drafts, and those grades were hypothetical and not recorded. (2) High rater variance. Despite multiple grade-norming sessions, TA scoring exhibited substantial between-rater variability. \n\n(3) Scalability. A single uniform human rating for all essays was infeasible given class size. (4) Consistency. Prior work reports higher internal consistency for AI-generated feedback than for human-authored feedback [23]; similarly, from our data, AI rubric-level scores were more internally consistent than TA scores (Section 6.2.2). Moreover, system logs show that 88.7% of rubric-level AI judgments matched TAs' final judgments (Section 6.3.1), providing convergent validity at the decision level. \n\nWe used GPT-4o in the scorer because it was the best-performing model on benchmark datasets when this work was conducted. A potential concern with LLM-based scoring is generation-evaluation bias-a model may systematically favor text that it produced. Prior work suggests this bias is most pronounced when the same model both generates and evaluates AI-written text [85].",
