      "snippet": {
        "text": "Teacher education programs present a unique context in higher education. AI tools and their implications must be addressed at three levels: teacher educators, teacher candidates, and the students in schools where the teacher candidates will be placed. Each level presents its own complexities. Teacher educators have a variety of needs depending on their current knowledge of AI, their teaching background, familiarity and comfort with educational technology, and the grade span of their assigned courses. Some teacher educators instruct in a specific subject area, while others focus on preparing teacher candidates for field placements and supervising them in practicum experiences. Professional development around AI for teacher educators must acknowledge these diverse backgrounds and needs. \n\nWhen teacher educators deal with AI in their university classes, they also face unique challenges. Effective pedagogy for AI use is still in its formative stages. When modelling AI with their students, instructors must consider to what degree AI applications merely simplify tasks such as lesson design or synthesizing information on a new topic (Lameras & Arnab, 2022;Salas-Pilco et al., 2022). A potential danger of generating pre-packaged lessons with AI is that teacher candidates may be deprived of developing fundamental skills in curriculum design. \n\nTeacher candidates may also need to consider whether these lessons are appropriate for the educational context and are in accordance with teaching and learning theories. \n\nTeacher educators who prepare candidates for field placements also must recognize that attitudes towards AI use vary widely among school boards and schools within the same board. \n\nA teacher candidate may be encouraged to experiment with AI in one placement, only to face quite a different scenario in a subsequent practice teaching round. \n\nBrock Education Journal 33 (3) Embracing Change \n\nIn our journey of facilitating workshops with university instructors, we noted how they embrace change and innovation differently. Discussions and presentations highlighted variations in their initial responses to AIEd. Some instructors emphasized the need for students to be involved in the \"creation\" experience and the creative process, citing Dewey's experience theory (Dewey, 1986). Others thought that these tasks could be offloaded to the software so that they could focus with their students on tasks involving higher-order thinking skills. Other instructors inquired if there would be a place for traditional lectures, essays, and readings in the new world of GenAI tools, while others compared these tools to the use of calculators in mathematics classrooms.",
