      "snippet": {
        "text": "Participants' feedback and their expressed desire for more professional development align with our reflections and the literature on the importance of workshops on AIEd with university instructors (Couturier, 2023;Kurz et al., 2022;Ross, 2023). Postsecondary instructors must be aware of the potential uses and abuses of AI in the many subject areas of the curriculum. \n\nOngoing professional development and discussions are vital to ensuring that faculty members, especially teacher educators, are prepared to address arising implications. These workshops are warranted in teacher education specifically so that teacher candidates are prepared for their practicum placements and future teaching. This is of significance because many teacher educators in the teacher education program are retired or former teachers who may not be as familiar with the latest innovations in technology compared to full-time professors and may need additional information on what schools are experiencing. These workshops are also highly relevant to sessional instructors who may not have regular access to the pedagogical supports offered by the university, hence the need for similar professional development opportunities. \n\nGiven university instructors' diverse backgrounds and concerns, a one-size-fits-all approach to professional development in terms of content and training strategies will not suffice to empower those instructors in navigating their needs and interests. \n\nImportance of (Critically) Addressing the Emerging Nature of AI Professional development opportunities for faculty and sessional instructors must adapt to the evolving nature of technology and current challenges. However, while it continues to be important to demonstrate new AI tools as they are released, professional development must not end with this but increasingly grapple with critical questions of how GenAI impacts the teacher's role in the classroom. The traditional framing of the teacher as a dispenser of knowledge is challenged as AI makes it easier for students to seek information through digital tools rather than relying on the teacher. These pressing questions are rising to the forefront, as evident with the release of GPT-4o as one of many examples (OpenAI, 2024). This tool (released at the same time as this paper) offers ample innovative opportunities for personalized teaching in which the learning experience focuses less on the traditional role of the teacher.",
