        "text": "It explores their perceptions of AI's impact, the desired changes in teaching and assessment, and the motivations driving these changes. The findings shed light on the specific curricular, pedagogical, and assessment adjustments proposed by educators, centered around learning with AI, higher-order thinking, ethical values, learning processes, and face-to-face relational learning, and highlight the potential influence of an 'ignorance effect' on perceived impact. Another study evaluated the potential of generative AI tools and the 4PADAFE instructional design matrix in enhancing education through a MOOC course for University of ESPE Armed Forces teachers [16], revealing that integrating these tools with the instructional design matrix is crucial for developing massive virtual classrooms and personalizing educational experiences. \n\nA review study has explored the potential of generative AI (ChatGPT-3.5) with 110 primary school pupils (8-14 years) across four classes in two schools [17]. Results showed GAI could personalize learning materials to align with pupils' knowledge levels, enhancing engagement. While promising for motivated learning and skill development, generative AI tools require refinement for impactful, inclusive, and sustainable implementation in school education, benefiting pupils, teachers, and management. Walczak and Cellary [18] has discussed the role and impact of generative AI systems in higher education, exploring advantages, potential threats, and necessary curriculum changes. It examines university students' usage and perception through a survey, highlighting the need for digital literacy and ethical AI use. Recommendations emphasize harnessing AI's potential while mitigating risks, aiming to stimulate policy and strategy development for relevant and practical education in the digital landscape. \n\nSimilar to this work, Mcdonald et al. [19] has provided an analysis of documents from 116 US research universities, revealing that the majority (63%) encourage GAI usage, providing sample syllabi (56%), curriculum, and activities (50%) for its integration. However, guidance for STEM-related activities was vague, and over half (52%) addressed ethical concerns, including diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI). The study cautions that extensive revisions of the pedagogical approaches recommended in these policies can become burdensome for faculty.",
