        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Communication is an integral component of establishing a strong school community (Laho, 2019). Communication positively impacts family involvement (Galindo & Sheldon, 2012), students' academic performance (Bergman, 2012;Galindo & Sheldon, 2012;Kraft &Dougherty, 2013), andattendance (Sheldon, 2007). Schools strive to establish partnerships with parents to support student learning. Strong communication is fundamental to this partnership and to building a sense of community between home and school. \n\nIn these changing times, schools should look to continually expand their skills to maximize effective communication with parents (Graham-Clay, 2005). Yet school -home communication is \"infrequent and unsystematic in most schools\" (Kraft, 2016, p. 15). \n\nParents indicate they would like to have access to better information from teachers and schools (Epstein, 2011). At a macro level, an Irish study of school principals during COVID confirmed the centrality of relationships and communication between school principals, teachers, and the wider school community (Murphy and Devine, 2023). Our interests lie more at the micro-and evaluation levels. Specifically, how did Australian school principals communicate during the early stages of the pandemic and how effective was this communication. \n\nPrior research suggests that parents generally prefer a combination of communication modes when it comes to school-home communication. For example, Hoover-Dempsey and Sandler (1995) found that parents value personalized communication that is timely, relevant, and allows for teacher interaction. Garcia and Lee (2016) found that parents appreciate a mix of electronic communication, such as e-mails and school websites, as well as faceto-face and printed newsletters. In a study of 1,349 parents assessing the frequency and importance of communication across several modes, Thompson et al. (2015) found that parents prefer e-mail communication, while Chen & Wang (2020) highlighted the growing popularity of mobile communication apps in school-home communication. \n\nIn examining parents' and teachers' perceptions and use of a newly adopted Learning Management System (LMS) for school-home communication, Laho (2019) concluded that most families have Internet access, and that parents and teachers are now very comfortable using digital tools to communicate.",
