      "snippet": {
        "text": "In examining parents' and teachers' perceptions and use of a newly adopted Learning Management System (LMS) for school-home communication, Laho (2019) concluded that most families have Internet access, and that parents and teachers are now very comfortable using digital tools to communicate. Although newer tools are available, e-mail and phones continue to be preferred for bidirectional communication (Laho, 2019). Indeed, technology-based tools for parent -teacher communication are becoming the preferred method of exchanging information between home and school (Smith & Johnson, 2017). A recent study of seventh and eighth grade parents in Arkansas revealed an overwhelmingly preference for school contact through e-mail, text messaging, and the school website (Maxey, 2020). Similarly, a Californian study found that the pandemic increased the use of technology for communication and for better parent partnerships' (Hirst, 2021). A more recent survey of parents and teachers in the northeastern U.S. produced paradoxical results. On one hand, respondents reported greater perceived connectedness with more traditional and \"warmer and richer\" communication modes (as opposed to colder and leaner modes as defined by Social Presence Theory and Media Richness Theory). However, the results confirmed that participants still have a strong preference for using e-mail, a supposedly \"cold and lean\" mode, for all communication (Solano, 2022). \n\nSo, while parents prefer a combination of communication modes, particularly electronic communication, when it comes to school-home communication, little is understood of how educational leaders communicate during a crisis.",
