      "snippet": {
        "text": "Integrating AI into a learning objective of critical thinking, students first generate and then critique AI-generated text. As a further step, students use this experience to develop prompting skills that can increase the quality of AI-generated content. Travis (2025) offers an interesting twist on this approach by considering not only the content of what is written but also the expectations of communication sources. Developed for a media literacy course but suitable for any course that considers speaker credibility or relationships, this unit asks students to discuss their reactions to personal letters without knowing if they were generated by AI or written by humans. The activity highlights an important focus of the Replication stanceon communication ethics, particularly on issues of integrity and authorship. \n\nThe third stance explores the potential of generative AI tools to Enhance typical human communication or capabilities. In these activities, generative AI adds new capabilities for communication or augments current capabilities. In their coauthored article, Rister and Velez (2025) each describe how they used generative AI tools to design two new courses (business communication and the history of communication technologies) not only in less time but also better informed by instructional design methods and principles. Similar to Lu's (2024) use of generative AI to create a realistic dialogue between two personas, Littell and Peterson (2025) demonstrate using a chatbot generator to create stakeholder personas that students can interact with as though they were real people as they are completing a stakeholder analysis assignment in a business or professional communication course. Activities adopting the Enhance stance also demonstrate how AI tools can support students who might otherwise struggle. For example, Heathco (2025) uses an AI-powered tool to create videos for students who speak English as a second or lower (L2) language by translating lessons from English and then creating animations of the instructor delivering the lessons in the students' native language (which, in this case, was Korean). In encouraging students to use generative AI tools for the familiar task of creating presentation slides, Camp and Johnson (2025) found that students with less graphic design experience (knowledge that was not part of course content) were no longer disadvantaged in comparison to their peers with such knowledge. \n\nIt is quite possible for activities to combine these stances or see them as blending into one another, depending on one's perspective.",
