      "snippet": {
        "text": "In the trace interviews, the teachers provided examples of their work with attendance checking, which in most cases was clearly influenced by their personal knowledge about and relationships with individual students. Some of the teachers mentioned that this process of providing the 'most accurate' documentation to report in the digital administration platforms needed some time, sometimes even spanning over two to three workdays. Therefore, teachers' responses to anticipated or unforeseen challenges with work on platforms, for example, in relation to the obligatory work of attendance reporting, typically involved several types of workarounds: 'temporary practices or solutions' that were enacted when events occurred unexpectedly and disrupted the 'standard or the expected workflow' (Wimelius, 2011, p. 43). In the above-mentioned examples, to counteract the tensions between monitoring and caring for students in the work of attendance reporting, teachers did several different and more profound workarounds, such as attending to the circumstances for individual students, creating one's own non-digital documentation and monitoring attendance in extended time periods. Hence, accomplishing digital attendance reporting work required additional work, increased work effort and a significant amount of work time spent daily on a similar and socially challenging task, which together adds to intensifying the work for teachers. \n\nAt certain critical points, daily attendance reporting was also challenged and caused extra work due to changes and updates to the platforms. Such interruptions were regularly experiences in particular for the public schools as they are required to do public procurements of platforms. The challenges often arose from the poor integration of the digital administration platform infrastructure with the school's other platform services. For example, at the beginning of our ethnographic fieldwork at the public Journeyman School, a new administrative platform for student registry information was implemented after a procurement process. The teachers' initial experiences with using this platform, as indicated in the trace interviews, were that the platform was not adequately integrated with the school's main school platform, which caused problems in terms of transferring student attendance reports and records between platforms. Teachers responded to these technical problems by doing workarounds.",
