          "Kevin Burden"
        ],
        "openAccessInfo": {
          "license": "CCBYNCND",
          "status": "HYBRID",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1080/1475939X.2024.2428601?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1080/1475939X.2024.2428601, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial Intelligence (AI) has become prevalent in nearly every aspect of our lives and has also penetrated the field of K-12 education through the use of various AI-powered tools. AI-based adaptive learning platforms, for example, can analyse performance data, identify areas of improvement and provide personalised learning experiences by adjusting the pace and content based on individual performance (Demartini et al., 2024). Similarly, Intelligent Tutoring Systems have the potential to provide personalised instruction, tailored content and feedback to students, and support teachers with assessment by providing automated grading and feedback systems (du Boulay, 2019). Furthermore, AI technologies are increasingly being used for education management and delivery. As Miao et al. (2021) explain, system-facing applications can be adopted to automate various aspects of school administration, such as admissions, timetabling, attendance and homework monitoring. For instance, the use of facial recognition technology can enable attendance management (Selwyn et al., 2023) while AI-powered surveillance systems that use video analytics can be seen to enhance safety and security by monitoring school premises (Tanner & McKelvey, 2019). \n\nAs AI use continues to permeate schooling, there is an emerging body of empirical research on how AI is used by teachers and students in K-12 settings. Recent systematic reviews of these studies report that educators use AI to support them with student monitoring, group management, automated grading and data-driven decisions (Crompton & Burke, 2022), as well as for machine learning demonstration and academic performance and behaviour prediction and intervention (Martin et al., 2024). The benefits of AI use for students are seen to relate to the use of AI Tutors for offering academic support similar to a physical educator, as well as the adoption of AI for aiding students in their thinking process and offering personalised learning tailored to their needs (Crompton & Burke, 2022). Furthermore, Martin et al. (2024) report in their systematic review that students used AI for scientific discovery learning and improving their learning experience. \n\nThe use of Emotional AI has also started to become present in classrooms, encompassing 'technologies that use affective computing and artificial intelligence techniques to sense, learn about and interact with human emotional life' (McStay, 2020b, p. 1).",
